              "exact_quote": true,
              "locator": "PDF page 25",
              "quote": "Claimants who have no partner (including self- funders)\n78088 An additional amount for the severely disabled is applicable to a person who is permanently in a\n\ncare home if all the conditions are satisfied1.\n\n\nNote: This will normally be self funding claimants, but DMs should be aware that it will also include\nresidents where the payment of “AA” or DLA/PiP continues for the first four weeks after admission.",
              "record_id": "https://chris-page-gov.github.io/okf-dwp/id/page/78/0025",
              "record_provenance_sha256": "3fcd94eb1fee455de0774041a0654493bc5c94d2a304f653c7056649b5d018d7",
              "record_text_sha256": "a1e2462f65812d22a647562270b9ebb3a3d64c4c76a205bd5cf6d243e607b193",
              "source_pair_in_selected_record": true,
              "source_url": "https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/69e10327f5069cdc54868955/dmg-Ch-78.pdf#page=25"
            }
          ],
          "claim_id": "C1",
          "explanation": "The heading, DMG 78088 and its four-week note support the main statement. The added scope text is too absolute about actual receipt and the absence of a carer receiving CA/UC: selected DMG 78060 contains treated-receipt rules and DMG 78036 a transitional-protection exception. The claim needs these qualifications or a narrower statement. The three named memos should not all be described as adding qualifying disability benefits: memo 01/26 changes carer-support terminology and related treatment.",
          "human_acceptance": "pending",
          "scope_or_qualification": "This concerns only the severe-disability additional amount in the no-partner branch, not the whole Pension Credit award. All qualifying conditions must remain satisfied, including actual receipt of a qualifying benefit, no non-ignored co-resident adults, and no carer receiving CA or UC carer element. Self-funding status alone does not settle entitlement. The note refers to benefit payment continuing for four weeks after admission; it is not a four-week rule for the whole credit. Dated changes in memos 02/25, 06/25 and 01/26 add qualifying benefits and are not consolidated here.",
          "statement": "Under the heading 'Claimants who have no partner (including self- funders)', DMG 78088 states that the severe-disability additional amount is applicable to a person permanently in a care home if all the conditions are satisfied. The accompanying note says this will normally be self-funding claimants, and will also include residents whose AA or DLA/PiP payment continues for the first four weeks after admission.",
          "support_observation": "core_supported_qualification_needs_revision"
        },
        {
          "citation_diagnostics": [
            {
              "exact_quote": true,
              "locator": "PDF page 25",
              "quote": "Claimants who have a partner\n78089 A member of a couple who is permanently in a care home is no longer a member of the same\nhousehold as their partner. In such a case each person may claim SPC as a claimant who has no partner.",
              "record_id": "https://chris-page-gov.github.io/okf-dwp/id/page/78/0025",
              "record_provenance_sha256": "3fcd94eb1fee455de0774041a0654493bc5c94d2a304f653c7056649b5d018d7",
              "record_text_sha256": "a1e2462f65812d22a647562270b9ebb3a3d64c4c76a205bd5cf6d243e607b193",
              "source_pair_in_selected_record": true,
              "source_url": "https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/69e10327f5069cdc54868955/dmg-Ch-78.pdf#page=25"
            },
            {
              "exact_quote": true,
              "locator": "PDF page 24",
              "quote": "77128 Where one of a couple is permanently in a care home or independent hospital (see DMG 77005 et\nseq), that person is treated as not being a member of the same household as their partner (see DMG\n77117 2.). But both partners, or all members of a polygamous marriage, may be permanently in a care\nhome or independent hospital. In such a case the DM must decide whether the partners, or members of\nthe polygamous marriage, are nevertheless members of the same household.",
              "record_id": "https://chris-page-gov.github.io/okf-dwp/id/page/77/0024",
              "record_provenance_sha256": "93a0ff91c5c02e5ace05cee6d03d91bef541c706df76ed226bc1bbc322f850a7",
              "record_text_sha256": "617b15668bd7233b6161a36d30d86201be5fdf6df9e702b84fb1b9733b40392e",
              "source_pair_in_selected_record": true,
              "source_url": "https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/68401a731d85c6606009cce4/dmgch77.pdf#page=24"
            }
          ],
          "claim_id": "C2",
          "explanation": "The two quoted passages support household separation, may-claim language and the both-partners qualification. Selected DMG 77129–77130 on the same cited page supports the fact-and-degree explanation. The reference to remaining entitlement conditions at DMG 77031 has selected support on Chapter 77 page14 but is not itself one of this claim’s explicit evidence citations.",
          "human_acceptance": "pending",
          "scope_or_qualification": "'May claim' does not mean an award automatically follows; the entitlement conditions in DMG 77031 still apply. Partner status after admission is not assumed. The both-partners branch is a question of fact under DMG 77129–77130. Temporary absence is a separate branch.",
          "statement": "Under DMG 78089 and 77128, a member of a couple who is permanently in a care home is treated as not being a member of the same household as their partner, and each person may claim State Pension Credit as a claimant who has no partner. Where both partners are permanently in a care home, the decision maker must decide whether they are nevertheless members of the same household.",
          "support_observation": "supported_with_additional_selected_text"
        },
        {
